  "thesis_summary": {
    "thesis": "Zimmer Biomet Holdings is a global musculoskeletal medical technology company selling hip and knee implants, sports medicine and extremities products, craniomaxillofacial and thoracic devices, and enabling technologies including surgical robotics, navigation, and bone cement. **[Fact]** FY2025 net sales were **$8.23 billion** (+7.2% versus $7.68 billion in 2024). **[Fact]** The company reports three geographic operating segments (Americas, EMEA, Asia Pacific) and product categories including Knees (**$3.32 billion**), Hips (**$2.09 billion**), and S.E.T. (sports medicine, extremities, trauma, CMFT, thoracic). **[Fact]** Cash from operations was **$1.70 billion** and capital spending **$224 million**, yielding about **$7.41** of free cash flow per diluted share on **198.7 million** shares. **[Inference]** ZBH is a large-cap orthopedic compounder whose economics depend on procedure volumes, surgeon preference, and hospital contracting, with growing exposure to robotics and extremities after the Paragon 28 acquisition.",
    "why_market_wrong": "There is **no clear dated mispricing signal** (HK predictive attribute: **none**). **[Inference]** The stock may embed skepticism about orthopedic volume cyclicality, EU Medical Device Regulation recertification costs, and leverage from the Paragon 28 deal while under-crediting robotics pull-through (ROSA Knee, Monogram autonomous knee) and management's U.S. direct sales-force transition. Return at today's price is **normalized owner cash flow**, not a catalyst with a single payoff date. Peer comparison (Stryker, Medtronic spine/ortho, Smith+Nephew) frames whether robotics and SET mix are already fully priced.",
    "stance": "watch",
    "archetype": "compounder",
    "base_irr_pct": 21.35,
